WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.689
If you want to hear about the undiscovered
rooms of the Nerve Gas House that we never

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:06.860
see in the movie, then stick around to the
end of this video.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:10.820
[INTRO]

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.500
“Greetings. And welcome...”

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.400
...to Things You Missed.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.619
I love how cheerfully he says greetings.

00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:17.910
Gets me every time.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:21.630
Director James Wan would step away from the
Saw franchise after the first film, and open

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:22.180
the door…

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.620
“SOMEBODY OPEN THE ****ING DOOR!”

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:28.180
...for a new face to take the reins on the
series, so Saw II would be directed by Darren

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.180
Lynn Bousman.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:32.579
Bousman would head up the next three movies,
and establish most of what we think of when

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:33.730
we think of Saw today.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:37.790
I’d consider the Saw saga to be more his
product than any other director, and he would

00:00:37.790 --> 00:00:40.469
return to direct the 9th installment, Spiral.

00:00:40.469 --> 00:00:44.550
With a new director came some new Things You
Missed, so grab your magnifying glass, and

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.550
let’s get into them.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.880
[THINGS YOU MISSED]

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.280
The movie opens the same way that the first
did: with a light.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.219
We see a bare light bulb come in and out of
focus and it suddenly shines very bright.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.300
In the first Saw, I said that the light represented
Jigsaw trying to enlighten his test subjects

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.420
and make them see the error of their ways.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.630
That concept took more of a center stage in
Saw II, making this an appropriate opening.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.580
Some of John’s trap designs throughout the
Saw series took ancient torture devices and enhanced

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:12.200
them with industrial advances.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:16.270
One example is the first trap of the movie,
the Venus Fly Trap, which drew inspiration

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:20.289
from the medieval contraption, The Iron Maiden,
only this was more like a cute little iron

00:01:20.289 --> 00:01:21.789
maiden for your face.

00:01:21.789 --> 00:01:25.469
When the victim watches his tape, the assistant
seen on screen has a bit of a limp, there’s

00:01:25.469 --> 00:01:26.779
a slight hobble to his step.

00:01:26.779 --> 00:01:30.039
If you’ve seen the twist at the end of Saw
7, you now know why.

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:33.649
If you’re about to correct me and say, “It’s
called Saw 3D or Saw: The Final Chapter,”

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:35.940
save you breath, I prefer to call it Saw 7.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.860
*hip-hop music*

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.499
Soon after we meet Eric Matthews, who is going
to pick up his son Daniel who’d been caught

00:01:41.499 --> 00:01:43.549
shoplifting at an electronics store.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:46.840
During this scene, the camera cuts away to
a shot of a security camera, which shows us

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.960
the reason that Daniel is so familiar with
security technology later on…

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:53.920
“Those types of cameras don’t have sound.”

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.560
And more importantly, establish one of the
main concepts of Jigsaw’s next game.

00:01:59.569 --> 00:02:03.110
Part of what makes the twist work is the fact
that we, as the audience, believe that what

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.799
we are seeing on these monitors is a live
feed.

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:09.119
On a side note, this is one of the only movies
to establish a relationship we care about,

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:13.190
the father son dynamic between Eric and Daniel,
before they both get tangled up with Jigsaw,

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.250
and it goes a long way to help make this the
most powerful entry in the series on an emotional

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:17.740
level.

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.880
Eric is called in to see the Jigsaw crime
scene that we saw in the opening because the

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:25.240
killer has called out Detective Matthews…
or rather, Detective Mathews.

00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:28.939
As brilliant as Jigsaw may have been, it looks
like he still struggles with spelling.

00:02:28.939 --> 00:02:32.629
We know that double-Ts is the correct spelling,
because that’s how the names appear in the

00:02:32.629 --> 00:02:33.629
end credits.

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:37.569
When Eric is back at the police station, many
notices and wanted posters cover the walls,

00:02:37.569 --> 00:02:40.980
while the shelves are filled with binders
and boxes of evidence files.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.269
One of these boxes is for a case about a victim
named Greg Copeland, a reference to the co-producer

00:02:45.269 --> 00:02:49.120
of Bousman’s initial three Saw films, who
also served as a production manager and second

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:49.920
unit director.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.720
Another box in that room lists a victim named
Charlie Taylor, who was the film’s weapons

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:54.220
wrangler.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.280
After Matthews has the realization about the
location of Jigsaw’s hideout, the SWAT team

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.360
makes an entrance strategy on the whiteboard,
which involved several smaller teams of men.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.930
The first team consisted of Black, Faulkner,
Wisman and Brady, which corresponded to electrician

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.280
Craig Black, key grip Chris Faulkner (or possibly
his brother Tyler), first assistant editor

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.870
Ron Wisman Jr. and art director Michele Brady.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:17.489
OK, enough of that boring stuff, you know
****’s about to go down when Jigsaw puts

00:03:17.489 --> 00:03:18.489
away his cereal.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:22.540
The main game of Saw II is the Nerve Gas House,
where 8 criminals must solve puzzles to win

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:26.040
the antidotes to the poison being pumped in
through the ventilation system.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.040
From what I’ve heard, the original scripts
of the film have detailed the specific traps set for

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.052
each character, but the final cut only shows
the location of 5 antidotes: the one

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.500
in the safe in the room they start off in,
the two in the furnace trap, the one in

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:39.900
the needle pit, and the one in the razor box.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.780
Of those five, only one game was played by
the test subject it was intended for, which

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:46.220
was Obi, who was challenged to go into the
furnace trap.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:49.740
According to the director’s commentary,
the razor box trap was intended for Gus,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:51.340
who doesn't make it that far because he

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.790
falls victim to the Magnum Eyehole very early
on.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.840
Normally I wouldn’t consider the information in the commentary track canon,

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.160
so I wouldn’t include something

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:01.440
like that in my Things You Missed, but we
do see that Gus is a businessman, and the

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.439
trap seems to have been created to punish
greed.

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:07.290
It’s not hard to imagine that Gus was chosen
by Jigsaw because of corporate greed.

00:04:07.290 --> 00:04:11.749
We more or less find out why Addison was chosen
in Saw 4, and the razor box trap doesn’t

00:04:11.749 --> 00:04:13.569
seem to fit the bill for prostitution.

00:04:13.569 --> 00:04:15.780
So who were the other antidotes supposed to
go to?

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:19.449
In my headcanon, the second needle in Obi’s
trap would have gone to Daniel, since he’s

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:23.252
just a kid, and you know all the usual cliches
about him having his whole life ahead of him

00:04:23.252 --> 00:04:24.252
and stuff.

00:04:24.252 --> 00:04:26.770
I’m not saying that the other prisoners
would have let that happen, but I think that’s

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:28.449
how Jigsaw drew up the game.

00:04:28.449 --> 00:04:32.650
The only other one we know for a fact is the
needle pit trap, which was intended for Xavier,

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.500
but he ends up throwing Amanda into the pit
to do his task for him.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:38.680
Speaking of which, I think we can subtract
one, because it seems pretty clear that because

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:41.370
Amanda was in on the game, she had already
been treated.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.570
This is supported by the fact that she never
runs out of breath or spits up blood as the

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:45.570
others do.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.460
So that leaves Laura, Addison and Jonas.

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:50.580
I think that the antidote in the safe would
have been for Jonas, because he seems most

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.220
interested in solving the riddle that Jigsaw left
for them on the tape, so it’s likely that

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.830
John knew he liked puzzles, and that would
essentially become his task.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.300
So that just leaves Addison and Laura.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.401
There are plenty of locked rooms in the corridors
that we never see the inside of, and some

00:05:03.401 --> 00:05:06.820
of them seem to just open on their own once
a certain amount of time had elapsed, but

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:09.889
neither of these characters lived long enough
to make it to their tests.

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:13.539
While we don’t see the inside of their rooms
in the movie, we can learn about the traps

00:05:13.539 --> 00:05:16.919
that may have been inside them if we take
a closer look at the design sketches pinned

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.840
up all over Jigsaw’s workshop.

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.860
John Kramer’s headquarters at the Wilson
Steel Plant seemed to be his go-to spot for

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.440
research and development.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.160
Taking a look around, the place is covered
with blueprints, design sketches and prototypes

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.610
for traps, which were tested on the mannequins
scattered around the facility, which were

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:45.340
probably brought over from his previous workshop
at Stygian Street, whose location had been

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:47.840
compromised by Detective Tapp in 2004.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.910
The designs include previous traps, like the
original reverse bear trap, which we saw Amanda

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.870
tested on during the first movie, or the Venus
Fly Trap from the opening of this movie.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:59.159
Many of the sketches represent the traps seen
at the Nerve Gas House, like the Razor Box

00:05:59.159 --> 00:06:00.310
and The Magnum Eyehole.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:04.330
If those are present, chances are we can also
see the two unseen traps from the Nerve Gas

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:06.530
House, the ones intended for Laura and Addison.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:09.690
There are a number of traps that can be seen
in the workshop that don’t correspond to

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.199
any trap that’s used during the series,
like this one, which appears to be some kind

00:06:13.199 --> 00:06:16.789
of skull cage, possibly with the intention
of impaling the victim’s head.

00:06:16.789 --> 00:06:20.680
Another variation on the idea shows up later,
and looks to introduce an electric shock component

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.759
to the design.

00:06:21.759 --> 00:06:24.599
Another design shows us some kind of gas mask
with a switch on the front.

00:06:24.599 --> 00:06:29.150
When we see more concept sketches later, one
of them is labeled as The Toxin Table, though

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:32.590
it is not known if that is meant to be used
in conjunction with the gas mask.

00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:35.169
This thing may or may not be the Toxin Table.

00:06:35.169 --> 00:06:38.300
Assuming the gas mask and Toxin Table do go
together though, I’d guess that is the trap

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.259
for Addison, I can see there being some kind
of analogy being made with toxins being compared

00:06:42.259 --> 00:06:46.400
to disease and the gas mask being compared
to protection as it pertains to the corner

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.530
girl lifestyle.

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.970
We don’t ever find out why Laura is tested
by Jigsaw, but she would have the last trap

00:06:50.970 --> 00:06:55.160
by default, be it the electric skull cage
thing, or one of these other prototypes.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.759
Another possibility is this football helmet
one, but there isn’t any evidence of John

00:06:58.759 --> 00:07:01.580
getting started with any preliminary designs
on that.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.050
There are other trap designs that I think
may be very early versions of some of the

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.990
other devices John would use later in his
career.

00:07:07.990 --> 00:07:10.020
Would you call serial killing a career?

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.430
I mean I guess in his case it’s more of a retirement
plan.

00:07:12.430 --> 00:07:14.140
“Hello investment firms…

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:19.500
I see you’ve fraudulently mishandled funds
for my… *laughter* for my 401K.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:20.520
I can’t do this.”

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:24.259
But yeah, right here I noticed a trap that
looks to be some kind of spinal cord device,

00:07:24.259 --> 00:07:27.810
which reminded me of the Spine Cutter trap
that Art Blank had to wear in Saw 4.

00:07:27.810 --> 00:07:30.949
There was also this mannequin with a series
of tubes running from the head, and I have

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:34.400
to wonder if that would be related to the
Oxygen Crusher that eventually appeared in

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:35.300
Saw 6.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.860
Outside of what we actually see in John’s
workshop, there is also a moment that I believe

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:41.050
inspired future traps in the series.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:44.509
When John is talking to Detective Matthews,
he describes the moment his mission truly

00:07:44.509 --> 00:07:48.690
began, when he tried to take himself out Bill Murray
Groundhog Day style and the way he describes

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:52.199
what he felt is an important step in how he
came to believe that his traps would cause

00:07:52.199 --> 00:07:53.729
people to appreciate life.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:54.729
Roll it.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:55.800
Off the side of a cliff.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.780
"To my amazement, I was alive...

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:04.240
...and I was determined to spend the rest of my days...

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.140
...testing the fabric of human nature."

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:10.931
The obvious trap idea to come out of this
would be the Horsepower Trap from Saw 7, where

00:08:10.931 --> 00:08:14.659
one of the victims experiences a
car crash from the same perspective John would

00:08:14.659 --> 00:08:17.620
have had when he threw his vehicle into autopilot
when there was no autopilot cause it was the

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:18.620
mid-90s.

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:21.949
But after the crash John can be seen pulling
these stakes out of his body, and I think

00:08:21.949 --> 00:08:26.400
that's what gave him the idea for the Spike Trap,
which he would use in Saw Si…-- four?

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.960
Guys, I am so good at reading Roman numerals…

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.990
...as this seems to be the part of the experience

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.590
that really made him feel alive.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:35.180
But enough about the traps, let’s talk about
the game.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.220
“This whole house is a trap.”

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:37.940
Touche.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.340
At the very beginning Amanda is asleep, face
down on the floor like she’s some kind of

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:42.600
Taking Back Sunday lyric.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:45.150
(Though if anyone’s a lyriq in this movie,
it’s Rigg.)

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:48.390
Amanda, who would be the facilitator of the
game at The Nerve Gas House, starts off in

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.430
the exact same position that John Kramer did
when he facilitated The Bathroom Game a year

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:53.060
prior.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.460
Eric’s son Daniel is wearing some band merch
for a group called Wrath of the Gods.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.070
Those of you who saw my Things You Missed
episode on the first Saw may remember a character

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:03.700
named Scott Tibbs, who was Adam’s childhood
best friend, who also went on to make his

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.350
own documentary about Jigsaw.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:09.080
Scott’s band in that documentary is none
other than Wrath of the Gods.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:15.160
“Scott Tibbs, lead guitar, founder of Wrath
of the Gods.”

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:19.760
Last week I said that The Scott Tibbs Documentary
was non-canon, but after watching it again,

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:23.540
there isn’t really anything that contradicts
the movies… so I’m not sure why Saw fans

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.040
consider it to be non-canon.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.270
I think it’s just because they don’t like
it.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.540
But outside of Daniel’s Wrath of the Gods
shirt being a reference to Scott Tibbs, it

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:34.380
also represents the cult of Jigsaw, who would
come to see himself as a God, so you could

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.490
say he brought his wrath down upon his many
victims.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:41.100
This wrath would not extend to Daniel himself
though, and Jigsaw gives a couple of little

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:42.830
clues towards the boy being safe.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:45.200
Or at least… being in a safe.

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:46.260
"Tell me where he is."

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:49.020
“He’s in a safe place.”

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.830
Also, later on when he promises Eric that
if he follows the rules, he’ll see his son

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.020
again, he darts his eyes down towards where
the safe is.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.660
"If you agree to that then you will see your son again."

00:09:57.970 --> 00:10:01.710
These clues given audibly by Jigsaw aren’t
the only hints that what the police are seeing

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.080
on the screens isn’t taking place in real
time.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.510
If we look at some more of the background
details of the workshop, we can find even

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:08.720
more evidence.

00:10:18.980 --> 00:10:22.820
When police discover the numbers counting
down next to the monitors, they assume it’s

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:26.570
a bomb, and later decide that it’s probably
a countdown to the end of the Nerve Gas game.

00:10:26.570 --> 00:10:30.520
However, if you do the math, which normally
I don’t like to do but will make an exception

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:34.780
for you, the viewers, the timer has over 4
hours to go, whereas the game was only supposed

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:36.380
to last 2-3 hours.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.540
"Three hours from now the door to this house will open.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:44.780
Unfortunately you only have two hours to live."

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:48.680
There are also a few items that are seen in
both the Nerve Gas House and The Wilson Steel

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:49.680
Plant.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:52.720
Items don’t typically exist in two places
at once.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.040
What I’m getting at is the events taking
place on the computer screens cannot be taking

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.040
place at the same time as the police raid.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:11:01.330
The wheelchair with the mannequin upon which
the note for Obi is found can be seen in the

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:02.600
room with the monitors.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:05.820
The tricycle that Billy the Puppet used to
greet the police when they first arrived at

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:09.630
the steel plant is also present in the hallway
over at the house and the X Marks the Spot

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:13.110
painting can be seen hanging in the background
in Jigsaw’s workshop.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:17.040
Earlier I mentioned briefly how Jigsaw developed
a God-complex, and this painting has a very

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.880
biblical look to it, like something you might
find in a church.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:23.560
He probably does consider his workshops to
be his churches, going so far as to light

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:26.560
candles to perform a ceremony on Amanda in
Saw 3.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.740
The signal that the Metropolitan Police’s
Tech Team leads them back to a different house,

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.130
where Rigg hits pause on the VCR to determine
that the game they had been watching on the

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.350
screens all along was already over.

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:40.600
One question you might have had is where Jigsaw
is getting all of these empty houses and buildings.

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.990
Fans of the franchise know that John Kramer
was once involved in a project called Urban

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:47.690
Renewal Group which helped provide housing
to low-income families, but he abandoned the

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:49.130
endeavor halfway through.

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:52.730
I think that the Nerve Gas House was going
to be one of these homes, but John reinforced

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.420
it to make it the setting of his game.

00:11:54.420 --> 00:11:58.000
The fake out house was probably a replica
or model home, which would explain why it

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.120
has the same layout and why Jigsaw was able
to trick the SWAT team.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.250
The twist in this movie works great because
as the audience, we are on the same level

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:05.560
as the protagonists: the police.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:09.510
We are being presented the events out of order,
because that’s how they are perceiving the

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:10.510
situation.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.130
We find out the truth when they find out.

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:16.350
Whereas, in a movie like Jigsaw, the story
is told the way that it is just to trick the

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:20.320
audience, there’s no other practical reason
for doing it that way and it feels artificial.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.350
Another thing that Saw II gets right that
you may not have thought about is the theming

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.650
of the traps to fit the character who is playing
them.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:28.340
For example, it seems the Needle Pit Room
was once a nursery.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:33.860
We see ABCs written on the walls, baby bottles,
toys, carriages and even creepy little infant

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:34.360
mannequins.

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:38.180
Xavier is revealed to have been a drug dealer
who took advantage of his clients’ addictions.

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.570
When a junkie becomes addicted, their bodies
become dependent on the drugs.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:45.670
Just as a baby in a nursery needs it’s parents to provide
for it, the drug addicts need to keep crawling

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.500
back to Xavier to get their fix.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.180
Desperation seems to be a key theme in the
film.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:53.540
Had Obi not been so desperate to get both
antidotes, he could have seen the nozzle to

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:56.610
turn off the flames of the Furnace Trap with
minimal burn damage.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:00.480
Had Addison not been in such a huge rush to obtain
the antidote in the Razor Box, she might have

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:04.460
noticed that it was already unlocked, which
would allow her to open it up and get what

00:13:04.460 --> 00:13:07.060
she needs without putting her wrists in harm’s
way.

00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:11.470
And had everyone just taken a moment to sit
down and think about the clues given by Jigsaw…

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:14.380
"The numbers are in the back of your mind.

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:19.051
The clue to their order can be found over
the rainbow.”

00:13:19.051 --> 00:13:21.930
...they could have figured out that each of
them had numbers written on the back of their

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:24.010
heads, each one with a different color of
the rainbow.

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:27.750
By arranging them in the order of Roy G Biv,
(Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Indigo

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:30.480
and Violet, they would have obtained the combination
to the safe.

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.550
The big one of course, is Eric Matthews, whose
desperation to find his son led him back to

00:13:34.550 --> 00:13:36.080
the ways of police “brutality”.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:39.180
All he had to do to get his son back was sit
there and talk with Jigsaw.

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:42.860
When the contestants of the game discover
the X, they find the photo proving that Daniel

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:44.490
Matthews is Eric Matthews son.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:46.550
In the photo, they’re at a New York Knicks
game.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:50.580
While the location of the Saw movies is in
an unnamed fictional city only ever nicknamed

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:53.810
The Fairweather State, there are a lot of
clues like this that it’s supposed to be

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:57.550
somewhere in the Northeastern United States,
like the license plates being similar to New

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:00.530
Jersey and the New York skyline in the
Spiral trailer.

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:01.720
"Enjoy it while it lasts."

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:06.220
At about 73 minutes into Saw 2 there’s this
3 minute stretch where there's just a whole bunch of

00:14:06.230 --> 00:14:07.690
horror references for some reason.

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:11.360
It starts when the tech team discovers the
location of the fake out house, which is at

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:12.820
237 North Hyde Crescent.

00:14:12.820 --> 00:14:16.910
Two three seven is an homage to the spookiest
number in all of horror: Room 237 from The

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:17.520
Shining.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:20.900
If you aren’t a big fan of The Shining,
you might think this is just a coincidence, but trust

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:24.230
me when I say it’s not, if you pay attention
you’ll notice this number come up over and

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:26.300
over in all kinds of horror films.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.110
The way Hyde is spelled makes me think it
could be a nod to the classic novel Dr. Jekyll

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.110
and Mr. Hyde.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:33.630
Then there’s another potential street name
homage.

00:14:33.630 --> 00:14:35.640
“Make a right on Alfred.”

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:38.870
It’s a bit unusual to see a first name used
as a street name like that, and the murder

00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:42.680
mystery house nature of Saw 2 leads me to
believe this was a reference to, perhaps the

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:45.450
most iconic horror director of all time: Alfred
Hitchcock.

00:14:45.450 --> 00:14:50.200
Another iconic horror director was Wes Craven,
and his 1972 film, Last House on the Left

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:52.230
would also be mentioned on this car ride.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.000
“Last house on the left.”

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:58.900
After this, we see the conclusion of the Nerve
Gas House game.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:00.240
“Come on. Let me in!

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.580
Let me in!”

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:02.930
“Let me in!”

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:06.660
As I mentioned in the last episode, one of
the staples of the Saw franchise is that there’s

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.510
always a character who plays dead in order
to get an upper hand.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:12.120
In this case is Daniel who plays dead to get
the jump on Xavier.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.740
I can’t show that, because it’s too gory
for YouTube, but look, someone finally flushed

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:16.740
the toilet.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:19.680
And that’s one of the last things I’d
like to compliment Saw 2 on.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:23.310
It decided which aspects of the first movie
would become staples of the series, and set

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.740
the tone that would be used for the next 5
installments.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:29.360
It goes back to what I was saying earlier
about this really being Darren Lynn Bousman’s

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:30.360
franchise.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.780
He departed after Saw 4, but handed the torch
to the production designer that he brought on for

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:37.570
his Saw movies: David Hackl, and David would
hand it off to the series’ editor Kevin

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:39.130
Gruetert to finish it out.

00:15:39.130 --> 00:15:43.790
So the team was all together as early as Saw
2, part of the reason that the tone is consistent.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:47.420
Saw 2 would establish Bousman as a director,
but the biggest box-office success in the

00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:50.070
franchise would be his next endeavor: Saw
3.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:54.300
So make sure you join me on the next episode
of Things You Missed by subscribing to CZsWorld

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:58.990
for new horrors every week, ringing that deathbell
for notifications and I will see you in the

00:15:58.990 --> 00:15:59.720
next one.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.880
Assuming we both survive.

